    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "body": "## What\n\nIn any **packaged/built** desktop app, a cloud session's terminal never connects: the renderer's Content-Security-Policy refuses the direct `wss://` connection to the control plane, so the terminal shows \"Connecting\u2026\" forever. Dev-mode (`npm run dev`) is unaffected, which is why this went unnoticed.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`frontend/vite.renderer.config.ts` injects a CSP meta tag with `apply: \"build\"` (built renderers only). Its `connect-src` is:\n\n```\nconnect-src 'self' http://127.0.0.1:* ws://127.0.0.1:* <posthog>\n```\n\nThe cloud terminal deliberately dials `wss://<control-plane>/api/cloud/v1/terminal?ticket=\u2026` **directly from the renderer** (single-use ticket auth; the WorkOS token stays in the Electron main process \u2014 see `frontend/src/renderer/lib/cloud-terminal-mux.ts`). That origin is not in `connect-src`, so Chromium refuses the socket before any packet leaves the machine. Every other CP call rides the main-process proxy and is unaffected \u2014 including terminal-**ticket** minting, which keeps succeeding while the attach never happens.\n\n## Evidence (staging, 2026-08-30)\n\n- A dev on a packaged build: 4 sessions (claude-code **and** codex), **2,600+ tickets minted, 0 consumed** (`ao_access_tickets`), zero `GET /api/cloud/v1/terminal` requests reaching the CP.\n- Same app via `npm run dev` on another machine: 18 tickets minted, **18 consumed**, terminal works.\n- In the packaged app's DevTools, `new WebSocket(\"wss://echo.websocket.org\")` fails instantly with no Network-tab row (classic CSP refusal); the same snippet succeeds in a dev-mode build.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd the cloud control-plane WebSocket origins to `connect-src` in the built CSP (baked defaults `wss://staging-api.aoagents.dev` / `wss://api.aoagents.dev`, extendable via the existing `AO_CLOUD_CONTROL_PLANE_URL` override at build time). PR incoming.\n\n## Follow-up worth considering\n\nTunneling the terminal WebSocket through the Electron main process (like every other CP call) would remove the renderer-egress requirement entirely, at the cost of a bigger change to the mux.",
